  <article>
    <h1>48 Hours in Santa Barbara</h1>

    <figure>
      <img
        src="/images/santa-barbara-hero.jpg"
        alt="Santa Barbara beach and Stearns Wharf with the Santa Ynez Mountains beyond"
      />
    </figure>

    <p>
      <strong>
        Two days of Spanish Colonial history, Pacific views, neighborhood
        wandering, and a taste of Santa Barbara wine country
      </strong>
    </p>

    <p>Santa Barbara can make a weekend feel longer than it is.</p>

    <p>
      Maybe it's the mountains rising abruptly behind the city. Maybe it's the
      red-tile roofs and white stucco buildings. Maybe it's the way State Street
      leads you toward the Pacific, where palm trees, beaches and Stearns Wharf
      suddenly make downtown feel like a resort town.
    </p>

    <p>
      Or maybe it's because Santa Barbara gives you so many different versions
      of California without requiring you to spend the weekend in the car.
    </p>

    <p>
      Historic buildings and paseos sit comfortably beside restaurants and
      shops. The waterfront is close to downtown. The Funk Zone has transformed
      former warehouses into a neighborhood of galleries, restaurants, murals
      and wine-tasting rooms. And the city's layered history remains visible
      rather than being relegated to a museum.
    </p>

    <p>You could fill four days here without difficulty.</p>

    <p>We're giving it two.</p>

    <p>That means choosing carefully.</p>

    <h2>At a Glance</h2>

    <p>
      <strong>Best for:</strong> Couples, friends, food and wine lovers, history
      enthusiasts, walkers, coastal travelers, and anyone who likes a little
      sophistication without needing a formal itinerary.
    </p>

    <p>
      <strong>The pace:</strong> Active enough to see the city, relaxed enough
      to enjoy it.
    </p>

    <p><strong>Stay:</strong> Two nights.</p>

    <p>
      <strong>The weekend:</strong> Downtown Santa Barbara, distinctive
      architecture and history, the waterfront, the Funk Zone, and a taste of
      Santa Barbara County wine—with enough room left to enjoy where you are.
    </p>

    <p>
      <strong>Good to know:</strong> Much of this itinerary works particularly
      well on foot. Once you're downtown or near the waterfront, leave the car
      alone whenever you reasonably can.
    </p>

    <h2>Where to Stay</h2>

    <p>
      For only two nights, location is more important than having every possible
      hotel amenity.
    </p>

    <p>
      <strong>Hotel Californian</strong> places you between the waterfront and
      the Funk Zone. It is the upscale choice and particularly convenient if
      food, wine and walking are important parts of your weekend.
    </p>

    <p>
      <strong>Hotel Santa Barbara</strong> puts you directly on State Street in
      the heart of downtown. This is a strong choice if you want the city's
      shops, restaurants and historic center immediately outside the door.
    </p>

    <p>
      <strong>Harbor View Inn</strong> shifts the emphasis toward the Pacific.
      It sits across from the waterfront near Stearns Wharf, making it
      particularly appealing for travelers who want the ocean to be the first
      thing they see in the morning.
    </p>

    <p>All three work for the same reason:</p>

    <p>
      They reduce the amount of time you spend getting to Santa Barbara and
      increase the amount of time you spend being there.
    </p>

    <h3>Check Santa Barbara Hotel Availability</h3>

    <p>
      Compare current lodging options and availability for your travel dates.
    </p>
